---

<GuideLayout
  title="Arch Distribution"
  description="Build a minimal, fully customized Linux distribution from Arch Linux. Maximum control over every component — from the package list to the ISO profile."
  difficulty="Advanced"
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="prerequisites">
    <h2>Prerequisites</h2>

    <h3>System requirements</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>A working Arch Linux installation</li>
      <li>8 GB RAM minimum</li>
      <li>50 GB free disk space</li>
      <li>x86_64 processor</li>
      <li>Stable internet connection</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Required knowledge</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Comfortable with Linux command line</li>
      <li>Familiarity with Arch Linux internals and pacman</li>
      <li>Basic understanding of the Arch Build System (ABS)</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="preparation">
    <h2>Preparation</h2>

    <h3>Install required tools</h3>
    <pre><code>sudo pacman -S archiso arch-install-scripts mkinitcpio squashfs-tools git base-devel</code></pre>

    <h3>Create the working directory</h3>
    <pre><code>mkdir -p ~/archdistro
cd ~/archdistro

cp -r /usr/share/archiso/configs/releng/ work/</code></pre>
    <Note>
      <strong>Note:</strong> The <code>releng</code> profile is the base used to build the
      official Arch Linux installation image. It is a solid starting point for a custom distro.
    </Note>
  </section>

  <section id="base-system">
    <h2>Base system</h2>

    <h3>Edit the package list</h3>
    <pre><code>cd ~/archdistro/work
nano packages.x86_64</code></pre>

    <p>Recommended base packages:</p>
    <pre><code># Base
base
base-devel
linux
linux-firmware

# Networking
networkmanager
network-manager-applet
wireless_tools
wpa_supplicant

# System utilities
sudo
nano
vim
git
wget
curl

# Xorg
xorg
xorg-server
xorg-xinit</code></pre>

    <h3>Create the customization script</h3>
    <pre><code>mkdir -p ~/archdistro/work/airootfs/root/
cat &gt; ~/archdistro/work/airootfs/root/customize_airootfs.sh &lt;&lt; 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

ln -sf /usr/share/zoneinfo/UTC /etc/localtime

echo "en_US.UTF-8 UTF-8" &gt;&gt; /etc/locale.gen
locale-gen
echo "LANG=en_US.UTF-8" &gt; /etc/locale.conf

echo "mydistro" &gt; /etc/hostname
echo "127.0.0.1 localhost" &gt; /etc/hosts
echo "::1       localhost" &gt;&gt; /etc/hosts
echo "127.0.1.1 mydistro.localdomain mydistro" &gt;&gt; /etc/hosts

useradd -m -g users -G wheel -s /bin/bash liveuser
echo "liveuser:liveuser" | chpasswd
echo "root:root" | chpasswd
echo "%wheel ALL=(ALL) ALL" &gt; /etc/sudoers.d/wheel

systemctl enable NetworkManager
EOF

chmod +x ~/archdistro/work/airootfs/root/customize_airootfs.sh</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section id="customization">
    <h2>Customization</h2>

    <h3>Add a desktop environment</h3>
    <p>Append packages to <code>packages.x86_64</code>:</p>

    <h4>KDE Plasma</h4>
    <pre><code>plasma
plasma-desktop
plasma-wayland-session
kde-applications</code></pre>

    <h4>XFCE</h4>
    <pre><code>xfce4
xfce4-goodies
lightdm
lightdm-gtk-greeter</code></pre>

    <h4>GNOME</h4>
    <pre><code>gnome
gnome-extra
gdm</code></pre>

    <h3>Configure autologin (live session only)</h3>

    <h4>SDDM (KDE)</h4>
    <pre><code>mkdir -p ~/archdistro/work/airootfs/etc/sddm.conf.d/
cat &gt; ~/archdistro/work/airootfs/etc/sddm.conf.d/autologin.conf &lt;&lt; EOF
[Autologin]
User=liveuser
Session=plasma
EOF</code></pre>
    <Warning>
      <strong>Warning:</strong> Autologin is only appropriate for the live environment.
    </Warning>
  </section>

  <section id="packages">
    <h2>Package management</h2>

    <h3>Create a custom repository</h3>
    <pre><code>mkdir -p ~/archdistro/repo
cd ~/archdistro/repo

repo-add custom.db.tar.gz</code></pre>

    <h3>Build and add packages</h3>
    <pre><code>git clone https://aur.archlinux.org/package-name.git
cd package-name

makepkg -s

repo-add ../custom.db.tar.gz *.pkg.tar.zst</code></pre>
    <Note>
      <strong>Tip:</strong> Create a meta-package to group all your custom packages into a single
      installable unit.
    </Note>

    <h3>Reference the custom repo in the profile</h3>
    <p>Add to <code>~/archdistro/work/pacman.conf</code>:</p>
    <pre><code>[custom]
SigLevel = Optional TrustAll
Server = file:///home/user/archdistro/repo</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section id="branding">
    <h2>Branding</h2>

    <h3>Plymouth boot theme</h3>
    <pre><code>sudo pacman -S plymouth

mkdir -p ~/archdistro/work/airootfs/usr/share/plymouth/themes/mydistro
cd ~/archdistro/work/airootfs/usr/share/plymouth/themes/mydistro

cat &gt; mydistro.plymouth &lt;&lt; EOF
[Plymouth Theme]
Name=MyDistro
Description=Custom boot screen for MyDistro
ModuleName=script

[script]
ImageDir=/usr/share/plymouth/themes/mydistro
ScriptFile=/usr/share/plymouth/themes/mydistro/mydistro.script
EOF</code></pre>

    <h3>Default wallpaper</h3>
    <pre><code>mkdir -p ~/archdistro/work/airootfs/usr/share/backgrounds/mydistro
cp wallpaper.jpg ~/archdistro/work/airootfs/usr/share/backgrounds/mydistro/</code></pre>

    <h3>Custom bootloader entry</h3>
    <p>Edit <code>~/archdistro/work/efiboot/loader/entries/01-archiso-x86_64-linux.conf</code>:</p>
    <pre><code>title   MyDistro Linux
linux   /arch/boot/x86_64/vmlinuz-linux
initrd  /arch/boot/x86_64/initramfs-linux.img
options archisobasedir=arch archisolabel=%ARCHISO_LABEL% quiet splash</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section id="iso">
    <h2>Building the ISO</h2>

    <pre><code>cd ~/archdistro/work
sudo mkarchiso -v -w /tmp/archiso-tmp -o ~/archdistro/out .</code></pre>
    <Note>
      <strong>Note:</strong> ISO generation can take a long time depending on the number of
      packages included and the speed of your internet connection.
    </Note>

    <h3>Verify the output</h3>
    <pre><code>cd ~/archdistro/out
sha256sum *.iso &gt; sha256sums.txt</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section id="testing">
    <h2>Testing</h2>

    <h3>Test in QEMU</h3>
    <pre><code>qemu-system-x86_64 -boot d -cdrom ~/archdistro/out/mydistro-*.iso -m 2048</code></pre>
    <Warning>
      <strong>Warning:</strong> Always test on real hardware before a public release. VM behavior
      can differ from bare metal, especially for hardware-specific drivers and UEFI boot.
    </Warning>

    <h3>Testing checklist</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Live boot succeeds</li>
      <li>Networking and Wi-Fi work</li>
      <li>Desktop environment launches</li>
      <li>Package manager (<code>pacman</code>) is functional</li>
      <li>UEFI and legacy BIOS boot both work</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="distribution">
    <h2>Distribution</h2>

    <h3>Prepare the release</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Write documentation and a changelog</li>
      <li>Verify licenses of all included packages</li>
      <li>Publish ISO and checksums to a reliable mirror</li>
      <li>Set up a bug tracker and community forum</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Archive the source config</h3>
    <pre><code>cd ~/archdistro
git init
git add .
git commit -m "feat: initial distro release"
git archive --format=tar.gz --output=mydistro-source-1.0.tar.gz HEAD</code></pre>

    <h3>Automate future builds</h3>
    <p>
      Once your profile is stable, set up CI to rebuild the ISO automatically whenever packages
      are updated. Jenkins, GitHub Actions, and GitLab CI all integrate well with
      <code>mkarchiso</code>.
    </p>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>
